{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>He took up his parable and said.</b> 'Alas, who shall live' — when he mentioned that Assyria would dwell among the Kenites, he said also that a time would come when Assyria would be answered and would pass with them. The meaning is the passing of those whom Assyria made dwell, for the Kenites dwelt with Israel. The meaning of the Aramaic translator is known, but it is not the usage of Scripture to mention עבר without נהר. Some interpret וצים as plural, from the root of וצי אדיר. Others say its meaning is like 'and ships of Kittim' (כי דמו לציים — for they resembled ships), for a man is terrified by their appearance:" "The meaning of <b>Alas, who shall live when El sets him down</b> is: concerning the king of Assyria, who set himself up as though he were El. Thus it is written in the book of Isaiah. Or its meaning is: who shall live from the decrees that El sets in the world through Assyria, for the king of Assyria conquered all the nations, for so it is written:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The comment appears to engage standard interpretations of 'וְצִים' in Num. 24:24; the last clause is rendered hyper-literally as the Hebrew syntax is compressed and the antecedent of 'their appearance' is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:19:26.246058385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}